namespace Linx {

/**

\addtogroup design

\section design-memory Memory management

By default, memory is allocated on available acceleration devices (typically GPU's)
in order to minimize the costly transfers between the host and acceleration devices.

\section design-efficiency Performance and efficiency

Efficiency is the branch of performance which focuses on minimizing resource wastage.
This includes minimizing data movement and using available cores fully.

Wherever possible, we have designed Linx following Zero Cost Abstraction principles
(e.g. compile-time polymorphism or inlining- and compiler-friendly code),
ensuring that abstractions do not introduce any runtime overhead.
To this end, the library makes heavy use of template and metaprogramming techniques.
Most notably, interfaces are implemented as mixins instead of abstract classes.

\section design-extensibility Extensibility

We refer to Linx as a laboratory instead of a library because it is design for extensibility.
Using templates opens the library for extension, in order to maximize compatibility with established libraries
and enable implementing additional features in user code without any performance cost.

*/

}
